    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 241755\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1055 AM MST Thu Sep 24 2026\n\n.UPDATE...18Z Aviation Discussion\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Scattered showers and thunderstorms will be possible again this \n  afternoon and evening, with activity mainly focused over the \n  foothills and high terrain features.\n\n- After drying out temporarily this weekend, rain chances will return\n  to the region late Sunday into Monday as tropical moisture gets\n  pulled northward into the Desert Southwest.\n\n- Near to slightly above normal temperatures will persist through\n  the weekend before noticeably cooling early next week.\n \n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /Today through Friday/... \nStorm coverage was much lower than high-res models indicated last\nnight which was mainly due to lower instability and a stronger \ncap in place from extensive cloud cover over southcentral AZ. \nLatest wv satellite imagery and area radars still show abundant \nhigh clouds streaming into AZ with showers and thunderstorms \npercolating over southern portions of the state. All of this \nactivity is developing ahead of a coastal low pressure system \nlocated west of the Baja California (not related to the hurricanes\nmuch farther south). As we head through this morning, several \nlobes of vorticity will progress northward out of MX and provide a\nfocus of lift for additional showers and storms this afternoon \ninto this evening. \n\nIt is difficult to pinpoint exactly where these vortmaxes will end \nup moving as some models focus convective activity over the western \ndeserts this afternoon while others bring storms into central AZ. \nEither way, there will likely be another round of widely scattered \nconvection this afternoon, especially over the terrain features of \nsouthwest AZ and the foothills and higher terrain areas NE of \nPhoenix. There is still around a 20-30% chance that storms form over \nthe lower deserts, but this will be highly dependent on if activity \nacross the higher terrain can congeal enough to produce strong \noutflows which could generate additional convection. Instability \nwill not be as high as yesterday (SBCAPE < 1000 J/kg) but there \nstill could be a strong storm or two which could produce locally \ngusty winds and patchy blowing dust. The coastal low will weaken \nand lift northward on Friday which will cause upper-lvl winds to \nshift out of the west and bring much drier air into our region. \nCoincidentally, PoPs will decrease below 10% across the entire \nregion on Friday.\n\nThe presence of the upper-low over the next couple of days promoting \nnear seasonal 500 mb hghts, coupled with periods of extensive high\nclouds will help keep temperatures near normal across southcentral\nAZ. Highs across the lower deserts of central AZ will likely \nremain in the mid to upper 90s both today and Friday. Temperatures\nalong and west of the Colorado River valley will be slightly \nwarmer with highs reaching the lower 100s. Overall, the HeatRisk \nshould remain in the minor category with only very small pockets \nof moderate. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /Saturday through Wednesday/... \nDry westerly flow is expected to persist through at least \nSaturday with large scale troughing remaining over the Pac NW \nwhile the subtropical ridge slowly shifts southwestward from east \nTX into the interior of MX. Due to the presence of the ridge to \nour east, 500 mb hghts are expected to peak around 586-588 dam \nSaturday afternoon. This will result in highs topping out between \n100-105 degrees across the lower deserts (4-6 degrees above \nnormal) under mostly sunny skies and zero chance of rain. From \nSunday into early next week, there does look to be a significant \ndegree of cooling as tropical moisture arrives and rain chances \nincrease. Highs will likely fall below 100 degrees across the \nlower deserts on Sunday and continue to cool to the upper 80s and \nlower 90s Monday through Wednesday.\n\nOur attention then turns to the tropical Pacific Sunday into early \nnext week where potential moisture from two weakening hurricanes \n(Polo and Odalys) will come into play. There is still a high degree \nof uncertainty as to a) how far north the residual moisture will be \nshed out ahead of these decaying storms, and b) where the plume(s) \nof moisture will set up. Another major question mark will be just \nhow deep and progressive a developing trough of low pressure will be \nover the Intermountain West. All of these factors will have to line \nup perfectly for our forecast area to get significant rainfall. \nBoth the GEFS and EPS are telling a different story with varying \ndegrees of moisture and timing and intensity of said trough which \nis really complicating things. The latest NHC track for Hurricane\nPolo shows the storm making landfall along the coast of the Baja \nCalifornia by Monday which if aligned with the passage of the \naforementioned trough would draw a large plume of moisture into \ninterior MX and and much of AZ. NBM PoPs have already increased to\naround 20-30% with an initial band of moisture on Sunday, but \nwith the main lift arriving on Monday, PoPs are now widespread \n30-60% across our forecast area. The remnant tropical moisture may\nlinger into Tuesday before fully exiting the region on Wednesday.\nIt is also worth pointing out that some ensemble members are \nshowing significant rainfall totals in excess of 1-2\" across \nsouthcentral AZ Monday-Tuesday but there is still significant \nspread between members. Therefore, we will likely have to wait \nuntil this weekend for things to become more clear. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1755Z. \n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nThe main aviation concern will be the potential for further\nSHRA/VCTS activity this afternoon and tonight. However, forecast\nconfidence is low regarding coverage an impacts as these next\nrounds will be highly conditional on how the rest of the day\nunfolds. With that being said, there is somewhat better confidence\nthat timing of possible overnight convection would fall sometime\nbetween 08-13Z, but even then, rain chances are only 20-30%. Due\nto the uncertainty, frequent adjustments/amendments may take \nplace over the next several hours. Outside of convective periods, \nwinds will follow familiar diurnal trends with speeds generally \naob 12 kt. SCT-BKN skies will be common through tonight, with the \nlowest CIGs remaining aob 6 kft. \n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nNo major aviation concerns are expected during the next 24 hours.\nOutside of a brief window of breeziness at KIPL this afternoon,\nwinds will be light and favor familiar diurnal directional trends,\nthough extended periods of VRB conditions will be common during\nthe forecast window. SCT to sometimes BKN skies will be present\nover the region until clearing takes by Friday morning. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nWidely scattered showers and thunderstorms will be possible again\nthis afternoon and early evening, mainly focused over the higher\nterrain of the eastern districts. Localized gusty and erratic \nwinds will be possible in the vicinity of any storm that develops.\nRain chances will decrease Friday into this weekend before much \nbetter chances for wetting rainfall arrives early next week. \nMinRHs will range from 25-35% today with a gradual decrease to \naround 15-25% on region-wide Saturday. Significant moisture return\nis expected across the eastern districts by Monday which will \nkeep RH values above 40% with excellent overnight recovery. Winds \nwill generally follow diurnal patterns with speeds around 10-15 \nmph with typical afternoon gusts in the upper teens.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Salerno\nLONG TERM...Salerno\nAVIATION...RW\nFIRE WEATHER...Salerno\n",
